A||||5||88CoupeV6||Everyone check this for safety :)||||9||
Z||000000||88CoupeV6||07-23-2001||10:16 PM||||If you haven't read <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/008948.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/008948.html</A>  yet you may want to. I just replaced the failed lines. They should be checked on everyone's Fiero. They were easy to replace. You can buy the steel line and connectors from most parts stores. Just bend them by hand. The brake lines come out of the proportioning valve and go under the spare tire area. You can lift the car if you have an aero nose or slide under without lifting if you have other noses. Just look at the line condition around the steering rack area and where they go into the area under the master cylinder. Mine were very rusty and when I had to brake hard 2 lines broke open and sent my car out of control  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I just fixed them easy once you remove the plastic spare tire well. Why did GM use mild steel? Why not stainless on such a safety device? Just like why the bleeders are not stainless so they jam in and corrode?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||9
Z||000001||rodmcneill||07-24-2001||02:04 AM||||Someone on Ebay recently was selling complete Fiero brake tubing in stainless steel for around $170.00-$200.00.||||reg||1
Z||000002||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-24-2001||11:03 AM||||I replaced mine a few weeks ago.. front to rear, borrowed the pipe bender (didnt need it)and the iso flange tool for a local parts store, they were really rusty and I was unsure of them after she sat for 3 years but they work great now.||||reg||7
Z||000003||Leadfoot||07-24-2001||11:50 AM||||No kidding!  The main line going into the rear brakes is notorious for breaking right before the T connector.  Luckily mine gave out when I was pulling into the garage. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierobaby||07-24-2001||12:40 PM||||dang I wish I would have known before my brakes shot out! my poor little 84 :-( my father was coming home form passing e-check and the brakes just completly went... I do not have a e-brake (cable was cut) so needless to say stop signs didn't work to well. thank god he made it home ok and now we are spending tons of money to fix my brakes. buit at least it didn't happen when I was in the car Iw ould have cried... I really hate the way the brake cables are though metric on one side and english on the other!? FREAKABLE!<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="LETTER"> AIM: Fierobaby87 <A HREF="http://angelfire.com/sports/sfiero" TARGET=_blank>http://angelfire.com/sports/sfiero</A>  </FONT><BR>          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cfiero.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by fierobaby (edited 07-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroNate||07-25-2001||01:53 AM||||I Personally about every 3 months take my car out and hammer the sh$# out of it on a straight back road, I nail the brakes as hard as I can at 60 then back off till the tires don't chirp. It has helped on several panick stops cause I know the car, but at the same time it severly stresses anything that can break, and with farm fields all around I can't harm much. but I think it is important to check these things, as part of my axle, engine and tranny upgrade I am putting corvette brakes on either 2000 brakes or 94 ZR1 HD brakes, no matter what I am putting an antilock system on, it should be interesting to get it to work but what the heck worse case I pull the electronics off and put on a newer 4 line master cylinder.<BR>Anyway glad to see that no one got hurt||||reg||1
